WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:13.582
Have you ever wondered why light casts sharp

2
00:00:13.582 --> 00:00:16.978
shadows even when sound waves bend around corners?

3
00:00:17.508 --> 00:00:19.368
Welcome to Ray Optics.

4
00:00:19.889 --> 00:00:24.392
In this chapter, we study how light travels and interacts with matter.

5
00:00:24.925 --> 00:00:29.806
Light is electromagnetic radiation with a wavelength ranging from 400

6
00:00:29.806 --> 00:00:36.116
nm to 750 nm, detected by the human eye.

7
00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:38.604
From everyday experience, we know light travels in

8
00:00:38.604 --> 00:00:41.770
straight lines and with an enormous speed.

9
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.949
Its accepted speed in vacuum is c = 3 * 10^8 m/s.

10
00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:51.604
Although light is an electromagnetic wave, its wavelength is extremely small compared

11
00:00:51.604 --> 00:00:56.282
to ordinary objects of size around a few centimeters or larger.

12
00:00:56.282 --> 00:01:02.413
Because this wavelength is negligible compared to macroscopic obstacles, light effectively

13
00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:06.747
travels in straight lines along directed paths called rays of light,

14
00:01:06.747 --> 00:01:09.747
while a bundle of such rays forms a beam of light.

15
00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:14.175
This ray approximation forms the foundation of ray optics.

16
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.735
We have completed Introduction and the Particle Model of Light.

17
00:01:21.278 --> 00:01:25.007
Now we start Reflection of Light by Spherical Mirrors.

18
00:01:25.528 --> 00:01:29.088
The angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence, and the

19
00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.971
incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane.

20
00:01:33.492 --> 00:01:36.778
A spherical mirror has a pole, centre of curvature,

21
00:01:36.778 --> 00:01:40.707
radius of curvature, principal axis and principal focus.

22
00:01:42.756 --> 00:01:45.685
For paraxial rays, the focal length is half the

23
00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:49.127
radius of curvature, so f equals R by 2.

24
00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:53.801
For a concave mirror, standard rays determine image formation, where

25
00:01:53.801 --> 00:01:57.563
standard rays parallel to the principal axis, through the focus,

26
00:01:57.563 --> 00:02:00.563
or through the centre form real or virtual images.

27
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.017
The mirror equation states that one by v

28
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:07.445
plus one by u equals one by f.

29
00:02:07.445 --> 00:02:10.493
Linear magnification m equals minus v by u.

30
00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.808
Check yourself.

31
00:02:14.327 --> 00:02:17.470
What does the focal plane of a spherical mirror represent

32
00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.494
in relation to rays parallel to the principal axis?

33
00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:30.548
The focal plane is the plane through the focus normal to

34
00:02:30.548 --> 00:02:34.560
the principal axis where reflected rays converge or appear to diverge

35
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.084
when the incident rays make some angle with the principal axis.

36
00:02:40.108 --> 00:02:43.979
We have completed Reflection of Light by Spherical Mirrors.

37
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.775
Now we start Sign Convention.

38
00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:50.796
According to the Cartesian sign convention, all distances

39
00:02:50.796 --> 00:02:52.953
are measured from the pole of the mirror.

40
00:02:53.522 --> 00:02:56.891
Distances measured along the direction of incident light

41
00:02:56.891 --> 00:02:59.953
are positive, and those opposite are negative.

42
00:03:00.475 --> 00:03:05.465
Heights above the principal axis are positive, and heights below are negative.

43
00:03:05.987 --> 00:03:09.308
For a concave mirror, the object distance u, focal

44
00:03:09.308 --> 00:03:12.727
length f, and radius of curvature R are negative.

45
00:03:13.248 --> 00:03:16.844
The image distance v is negative for a real image in front

46
00:03:16.844 --> 00:03:20.608
of the mirror, but positive for a virtual image behind it.

47
00:03:21.141 --> 00:03:24.106
For a convex mirror, f and R are positive.

48
00:03:26.136 --> 00:03:28.234
Let us work out a numerical.

49
00:03:28.753 --> 00:03:31.396
An object is placed thirty cm in front of

50
00:03:31.396 --> 00:03:34.195
a concave mirror of focal length twenty cm.

51
00:03:34.717 --> 00:03:37.907
Here object distance u is minus thirty cm,

52
00:03:37.907 --> 00:03:40.803
and focal length f is minus twenty cm.

53
00:03:41.348 --> 00:03:44.884
Using the mirror equation one over v plus one over u

54
00:03:44.884 --> 00:03:48.776
equals one over f, we substitute the values to find v.

55
00:03:48.776 --> 00:03:53.312
Solving this gives image distance v equals minus sixty cm, meaning the

56
00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:57.148
image is real and formed sixty cm in front of the mirror.

57
00:03:57.705 --> 00:04:01.693
The magnification m equals minus v over u, which equals

58
00:04:01.693 --> 00:04:06.181
minus two, meaning the image is real, inverted, and magnified.

59
00:04:08.204 --> 00:04:10.599
We have completed Sign Convention.

60
00:04:11.118 --> 00:04:14.073
Now we start Refraction and Snell's Law.

61
00:04:14.594 --> 00:04:16.309
Have you ever seen a straw look

62
00:04:16.309 --> 00:04:18.133
bent in a glass of water?

63
00:04:18.654 --> 00:04:20.916
When a light beam crosses a transparent

64
00:04:20.916 --> 00:04:24.264
interface, its direction of propagation changes.

65
00:04:24.785 --> 00:04:27.775
This phenomenon is called refraction of light.

66
00:04:28.285 --> 00:04:31.297
The refractive index n is defined as the speed of

67
00:04:31.297 --> 00:04:34.321
light in vacuum divided by its speed in the medium.

68
00:04:36.352 --> 00:04:39.162
Snell's law states that the ratio of the sine of the

69
00:04:39.162 --> 00:04:42.126
angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction

70
00:04:42.126 --> 00:04:45.019
is constant for a given pair of media and a given

71
00:04:45.019 --> 00:04:49.640
colour of light, and equals the relative refractive index n21.

72
00:04:50.171 --> 00:04:53.052
The two laws of refraction are that the incident ray,

73
00:04:53.052 --> 00:04:56.492
refracted ray, and normal all lie in the same plane,

74
00:04:56.492 --> 00:04:59.400
and that sin i by sin r is constant.

75
00:04:59.933 --> 00:05:02.909
In the famous lifeguard story, a lifeguard on the

76
00:05:02.909 --> 00:05:05.731
beach running to save a drowning child must balance

77
00:05:05.731 --> 00:05:09.162
running fast on sand versus swimming slower in water.

78
00:05:09.683 --> 00:05:12.409
By Fermat's principle, light takes the path

79
00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:14.659
of least time, which yields Snell's law.

80
00:05:16.668 --> 00:05:19.811
Light bends towards the normal in an optically denser

81
00:05:19.811 --> 00:05:22.930
medium because its speed decreases, and away from the

82
00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:26.325
normal in a rarer medium where its speed increases.

83
00:05:26.844 --> 00:05:29.525
Let us work out a numerical example.

84
00:05:30.047 --> 00:05:33.880
Light enters glass of refractive index 1.5 from air

85
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.597
at an angle of incidence of 30 degrees.

86
00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:42.977
Using Snell's law, sin 30 degrees divided by sin r equals 1.5.

87
00:05:43.499 --> 00:05:47.570
Since sin 30 degrees is 0.5, sin r equals

88
00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:51.963
0.5 divided by 1.5, which is 1 by 3,

89
00:05:51.963 --> 00:05:56.118
giving an angle of refraction r of 19.47 degrees.

90
00:05:58.136 --> 00:05:59.412
Check yourself.

91
00:05:59.931 --> 00:06:02.836
Why does a ray of light bend towards the normal when

92
00:06:02.836 --> 00:06:06.312
it enters an optically denser medium from a rarer medium?

93
00:06:13.332 --> 00:06:16.130
Light bends towards the normal because its speed

94
00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:19.225
decreases upon entering the optically denser medium.

95
00:06:21.272 --> 00:06:24.262
We have completed Refraction and Snell's Law.

96
00:06:24.782 --> 00:06:28.796
Now we start Total Internal Reflection and the Critical Angle.

97
00:06:29.329 --> 00:06:32.365
Have you ever looked into a swimming pool and noticed how the

98
00:06:32.365 --> 00:06:36.272
water surface acts like a perfect mirror when viewed from underneath?

99
00:06:36.805 --> 00:06:39.401
When light travels from a denser medium to a

100
00:06:39.401 --> 00:06:42.391
rarer medium, it bends away from the normal.

101
00:06:42.912 --> 00:06:45.484
As the angle of incidence increases, the

102
00:06:45.484 --> 00:06:48.439
angle of refraction reaches ninety degrees.

103
00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.508
The two conditions for total internal reflection are that light must

104
00:06:52.508 --> 00:06:55.424
go from a denser to a rarer medium, and the angle

105
00:06:55.424 --> 00:06:58.258
of incidence must be greater than the critical angle.

106
00:07:00.268 --> 00:07:03.044
Let us define the critical angle formally.

107
00:07:03.575 --> 00:07:06.135
The critical angle is the angle of incidence in

108
00:07:06.135 --> 00:07:08.849
the denser medium for which the angle of refraction

109
00:07:08.849 --> 00:07:11.399
in the rarer medium is ninety degrees.

110
00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.385
From Snell law, the refractive index n21 equals the sine of the

111
00:07:16.385 --> 00:07:20.566
angle of incidence divided by the sine of the angle of refraction.

112
00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:24.135
When the angle of incidence equals the critical angle

113
00:07:24.135 --> 00:07:27.506
ic, the angle of refraction is ninety degrees.

114
00:07:28.028 --> 00:07:32.444
Since the sine of ninety degrees is one, the equation simplifies to

115
00:07:32.444 --> 00:07:36.051
the sine of ic equals one divided by n, where n is

116
00:07:36.051 --> 00:07:39.980
the refractive index of the denser medium relative to the rarer medium.

117
00:07:42.024 --> 00:07:46.036
Let us examine Table 9.1 for various transparent media

118
00:07:46.036 --> 00:07:49.143
in air, using sin C = 1 by n.

119
00:07:49.143 --> 00:07:52.334
Water with a refractive index of 1.33

120
00:07:52.334 --> 00:07:56.169
has a critical angle of 48.75 degrees.

121
00:07:56.688 --> 00:08:01.500
Crown glass with 1.52 has 41.14 degrees.

122
00:08:02.034 --> 00:08:07.155
Dense flint glass with 1.62 has 37.31 degrees.

123
00:08:07.688 --> 00:08:11.724
Finally, diamond with a refractive index of 2.42 has

124
00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:16.405
an exceptionally small critical angle of 24.41 degrees.

125
00:08:16.926 --> 00:08:19.822
Why does a diamond sparkle so brilliantly?

126
00:08:20.343 --> 00:08:24.057
Because its critical angle is so small, most light entering

127
00:08:24.057 --> 00:08:27.260
the diamond strikes the cut facets at angles greater than

128
00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:31.807
the critical angle and suffers multiple total internal reflections, remaining

129
00:08:31.807 --> 00:08:35.736
trapped inside until it emerges intensely from the top faces.

130
00:08:37.772 --> 00:08:39.048
Check yourself.

131
00:08:39.567 --> 00:08:42.924
What two essential conditions must be met for total internal

132
00:08:42.924 --> 00:08:46.305
reflection to occur when light travels between two media?

133
00:08:53.352 --> 00:08:56.447
The light must travel from an optically denser medium to

134
00:08:56.447 --> 00:08:59.173
a rarer medium, and the angle of incidence in the

135
00:08:59.173 --> 00:09:02.185
denser medium must be greater than the critical angle.

136
00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.437
We have completed Total Internal Reflection and the Critical Angle.

137
00:09:08.968 --> 00:09:13.899
Now we start Total Internal Reflection: Demonstration and Applications.

138
00:09:14.432 --> 00:09:16.670
Have you ever seen shimmering puddles on a

139
00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.232
hot road that vanish as you approach?

140
00:09:19.765 --> 00:09:22.553
Let us explore total internal reflection.

141
00:09:23.087 --> 00:09:27.182
First, a laser pointer directed into slightly milky water in

142
00:09:27.182 --> 00:09:31.075
a glass beaker demonstrates partial reflection and refraction at the

143
00:09:31.075 --> 00:09:35.137
surface, and total internal reflection at larger angles.

144
00:09:35.658 --> 00:09:38.896
Second, a mirage occurs on hot summer days when

145
00:09:38.896 --> 00:09:41.099
layers of hot air near the ground have a

146
00:09:41.099 --> 00:09:44.244
lower refractive index than cooler air above.

147
00:09:44.765 --> 00:09:48.325
Light rays from a distant object bend progressively away from

148
00:09:48.325 --> 00:09:52.444
the normal, undergoing total internal reflection and making the sky

149
00:09:52.444 --> 00:09:54.730
appear as a pool of water on the ground.

150
00:09:56.776 --> 00:10:00.181
Totally reflecting prisms use glass with critical angle less

151
00:10:00.181 --> 00:10:03.526
than 45 degrees to turn light by 90 degrees

152
00:10:03.526 --> 00:10:07.397
or 180 degrees through total internal reflection.

153
00:10:07.928 --> 00:10:11.774
Next, optical fibres consist of a central core of higher

154
00:10:11.774 --> 00:10:16.074
refractive index surrounded by cladding of lower refractive index.

155
00:10:16.595 --> 00:10:20.559
Light enters the core and undergoes repeated total internal reflection

156
00:10:20.559 --> 00:10:24.562
along the entire length without appreciable loss of intensity.

157
00:10:25.107 --> 00:10:28.881
This technology is widely used in high speed communication

158
00:10:28.881 --> 00:10:32.845
and in endoscopy for medical examination of internal organs.

159
00:10:34.884 --> 00:10:36.160
Check yourself.

160
00:10:36.679 --> 00:10:38.965
Why does a mirage appear as a shimmering

161
00:10:38.965 --> 00:10:40.929
pool of water on a hot summer road?

162
00:10:47.968 --> 00:10:51.670
A mirage occurs because layers of hot air near the ground have

163
00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:55.825
a lower refractive index than the cooler air above, causing light rays

164
00:10:55.825 --> 00:11:00.158
from the sky to bend progressively and undergo total internal reflection.

165
00:11:02.196 --> 00:11:07.365
We have completed Total Internal Reflection: Demonstration and Applications.

166
00:11:07.896 --> 00:11:11.398
Now we start Refraction at a Spherical Surface.

167
00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:14.491
Have you ever looked through a crystal paperweight and

168
00:11:14.491 --> 00:11:18.113
noticed how objects inside seem distorted or shifted?

169
00:11:18.622 --> 00:11:21.646
Today we study refraction at a spherical surface,

170
00:11:21.646 --> 00:11:24.875
which forms the foundation for understanding lenses.

171
00:11:25.396 --> 00:11:28.586
When light travels from a medium of refractive index

172
00:11:28.586 --> 00:11:31.801
n_1 to another of refractive index n_2 across a

173
00:11:31.801 --> 00:11:35.089
curved boundary, it bends according to Snell's law.

174
00:11:35.598 --> 00:11:39.313
Let us define the key terms: O is the point object,

175
00:11:39.313 --> 00:11:42.741
I is the point image formed by the refracted rays, P

176
00:11:42.741 --> 00:11:45.610
is the pole which is the geometric centre of the spherical

177
00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:49.396
surface, and C is the centre of curvature whose distance from

178
00:11:49.396 --> 00:11:51.420
P is the radius of curvature R.

179
00:11:53.456 --> 00:11:56.694
To relate object distance u and image distance v for a

180
00:11:56.694 --> 00:12:00.646
single spherical surface, we use the formula n_2 by v minus

181
00:12:00.646 --> 00:12:04.149
n_1 by u equals n_2 minus n_1 by R.

182
00:12:04.668 --> 00:12:08.382
Here n_1 and n_2 are the refractive indices of the first and

183
00:12:08.382 --> 00:12:12.680
second media, u is the object distance, v is the image distance,

184
00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:16.311
and R is the radius of curvature, all measured with the Cartesian

185
00:12:16.311 --> 00:12:20.432
sign convention where distances along incident light are positive.

186
00:12:20.954 --> 00:12:25.751
In the derivation of this formula, we apply the paraxial approximation:

187
00:12:25.751 --> 00:12:28.858
we assume that the incident rays are close to the principal

188
00:12:28.858 --> 00:12:32.358
axis and make very small angles with it, so that we

189
00:12:32.358 --> 00:12:36.835
can approximate sines and tangents of angles by the angles themselves,

190
00:12:36.835 --> 00:12:40.382
allowing us to use geometry of similar triangles cleanly.

191
00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:45.186
We have completed Refraction at a Spherical Surface.

192
00:12:45.186 --> 00:12:48.553
Now we start Light Sources and Photometry.

193
00:12:48.553 --> 00:12:52.660
Have you ever wondered why a 100 watt bulb

194
00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.160
feels so much brighter than a 40 watt bulb?

195
00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:59.755
Photometry measures light as the human eye perceives it, rather

196
00:12:59.755 --> 00:13:02.731
than raw radiant power, because our eyes are sensitive only

197
00:13:02.731 --> 00:13:08.722
to wavelengths between 400 nm and 750 nm.

198
00:13:09.255 --> 00:13:12.553
Luminous flux is the total radiant energy emitted per

199
00:13:12.553 --> 00:13:16.305
second weighted by eye sensitivity, measured in lumen.

200
00:13:16.827 --> 00:13:20.434
Luminous intensity is the luminous flux emitted per unit

201
00:13:20.434 --> 00:13:23.874
solid angle in a given direction, measured in candela.

202
00:13:25.896 --> 00:13:29.420
When light shines on a surface, illuminance is defined as

203
00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:33.529
the luminous flux falling per unit area, measured in lux.

204
00:13:34.048 --> 00:13:37.167
According to the inverse square law, illuminance from a

205
00:13:37.167 --> 00:13:40.167
point source is proportional to 1 by r squared,

206
00:13:40.167 --> 00:13:42.491
where r is the distance from the source.

207
00:13:43.013 --> 00:13:45.858
E equals I divided by r squared, where E

208
00:13:45.858 --> 00:13:49.215
is illuminance in lux, I is luminous intensity in

209
00:13:49.215 --> 00:13:52.027
candela, and r is distance in meters.

210
00:13:52.548 --> 00:13:55.989
This happens because light spreads out over a sphere whose

211
00:13:55.989 --> 00:13:58.786
surface area grows with the square of the radius.

212
00:14:00.836 --> 00:14:02.112
Check yourself.

213
00:14:02.631 --> 00:14:05.238
Why does photometry measure light based on the

214
00:14:05.238 --> 00:14:07.596
human eye rather than raw radiant power?

215
00:14:14.616 --> 00:14:18.330
Photometry measures light as the human eye perceives it because our eyes

216
00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:25.021
are sensitive only to specific wavelengths between 400 nm and 750 nm.

217
00:14:27.068 --> 00:14:30.201
We have completed Light Sources and Photometry.

218
00:14:30.732 --> 00:14:33.401
Now we start Refraction by a Lens.

219
00:14:33.923 --> 00:14:37.628
Have you ever used a magnifying glass to read tiny print?

220
00:14:38.173 --> 00:14:41.268
A lens is a transparent optical medium bounded by

221
00:14:41.268 --> 00:14:44.568
two surfaces, at least one of which is spherical.

222
00:14:45.089 --> 00:14:48.363
A convex lens is thicker at the centre and converges a

223
00:14:48.363 --> 00:14:52.018
parallel beam of light to its principal focus, whereas a concave

224
00:14:52.018 --> 00:14:55.282
lens is thinner at the centre and diverges light.

225
00:14:55.816 --> 00:14:57.756
The focal length is the distance from

226
00:14:57.756 --> 00:15:00.197
the optical centre to the principal focus.

227
00:15:02.224 --> 00:15:05.653
The thin lens formula relates object distance, image

228
00:15:05.653 --> 00:15:08.893
distance, and focal length for any thin lens.

229
00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.424
It is stated as 1 by v minus

230
00:15:11.424 --> 00:15:13.664
1 by u equals 1 by f.

231
00:15:14.174 --> 00:15:17.507
Here, u is the object distance, v is the image

232
00:15:17.507 --> 00:15:20.864
distance, and f is the focal length, all measured in

233
00:15:20.864 --> 00:15:24.472
centimetres from the optical centre using the sign convention.

234
00:15:26.508 --> 00:15:31.010
How do lens designers choose curvatures to get a desired focal length?

235
00:15:31.529 --> 00:15:34.672
The lens maker's formula is 1 by f equals n

236
00:15:34.672 --> 00:15:38.413
minus 1 times 1 by R1 minus 1 by R2.

237
00:15:38.946 --> 00:15:42.672
Here, n is the refractive index of the lens material relative

238
00:15:42.672 --> 00:15:46.029
to the surrounding medium, and R1 and R2 are the radii

239
00:15:46.029 --> 00:15:49.637
of curvature of the two spherical surfaces in centimetres.

240
00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:54.870
Linear magnification is given by m equals v by u,

241
00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.466
which is the ratio of image height to object height.

242
00:15:57.466 --> 00:16:00.201
Let us solve a numerical example.

243
00:16:00.201 --> 00:16:05.356
Consider an object placed 30 cm from a convex

244
00:16:05.356 --> 00:16:08.690
lens of focal length 10 cm, so u is

245
00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:13.856
minus 30 cm and f is plus 10 cm.

246
00:16:13.856 --> 00:16:17.320
Using the thin lens formula, 1 by v minus 1 by minus

247
00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:22.749
30 equals 1 by 10, giving v equal to 15 cm.

